VERDICT -- rfnoise
==================
FOUR OF FIVE PART-A CELLS ARE NOISE.  HEAD's module, three draws each, against the single draw the
edited module gave in rfship part A:

              HEAD three draws                        spread   edit          verdict
  P21   1,014,768 /  1,087,112 /  1,076,570            7.13%   1,087,112     inside
  P4    2,813,285 /  2,681,096 /  2,681,096            4.93%   2,713,254     inside
  P2   59,860,379 / 59,860,379 / 60,754,481            1.49%  60,602,795     inside
  P9    5,847,680 /  5,755,343 /  5,817,115            1.60%   5,755,343     inside
  P34     250,125 /    249,163 /    249,163            0.39%     205,205     OUTSIDE -17.6%

On P21 and P9 the edited build returned a number HEAD itself produces, to the digit and with
identical components.  P2 looked reproducible on two draws and was not: the third scattered and the
edit fell inside.  That is the same trap P1 set in gaterf, where three identical draws were read as
convergence and the fourth landed 682,856.  TWO IDENTICAL DRAWS ARE NOT EVIDENCE OF DETERMINISM.

P34 IS NOT EXPLAINED AND IS NOT A LEAK.  peak_util 0.984600786705 < 1.00, so the gate condition is
false and the changed literal is never read; both modules execute identical bytecode under identical
environments.  A real difference here would indict my model of the run, not the gate.  The untested
asymmetry is that HEAD had three draws and the edit had one; the natural reading is that P34 is
bimodal -- a Z1=4 basin and a better Z1=0 basin -- and three HEAD draws landed together.  harness/
p34.sh runs three draws of EACH module to settle it and is queued behind rounds05.

WHAT THIS COSTS THE REST OF THE STUDY.  The noise floor at a 60 s limit is 0.4% to 7.1% depending
on instance, and rfship measured 17.96% on P34's own part-A pair earlier in the day under different
machine load.  Every conclusion in results/audit/ that rests on one or two 60 s draws sits inside
that band and should be re-read as unproven rather than settled.
